      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hamill was originally named Roseland before being renamed for Gail Hamill, a pioneer merchant, postmaster, and banker in town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamill%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Carrie Ingalls, sister of author Laura Ingalls Wilder, managed the local newspaper called the Roseland Review in 1910.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hamill_south_dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hamill lies along No Moccasin Creek, a tributary of the White River, with a dam nearby forming the Hamill Dam Reservoir.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamill%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town of Hamill was established in April 1909 as a townsite on the east side of No Moccasin Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hamill_south_dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Economic growth in Hamill was limited after the Chicago, Burlington and Quincy Railroad failed to build planned tracks through the area.",
